From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Andrew Gazelka <andrew@ix.dev>
Date: Wed, 22 Jul 2026 02:03:58 -0700
Subject: [PATCH] libexpr: force deterministic Wasm execution in builtins.wasm

The Wasm spec leaves NaN payload bits and relaxed-SIMD lowering
implementation-defined; wasmtime therefore does not canonicalize by
default, and upstream PR 15380 left it that way after measuring ~3.6x
on a float-heavy benchmark. Our evaluator results cross machines (eval
caches, fleet-shared store paths), where a single divergent bit means
divergent hashes, so enable cranelift NaN canonicalization and
deterministic relaxed SIMD unconditionally: eval-time plugins are
string/AST-shaped and do not pay the float penalty.

Assisted-by: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>

diff --git a/src/libexpr/primops/wasm.cc b/src/libexpr/primops/wasm.cc
index b1bd235..c0d2ffa 100644
--- a/src/libexpr/primops/wasm.cc
+++ b/src/libexpr/primops/wasm.cc
@@ -24,6 +24,17 @@ static Engine & getEngine()
         wasmtime::Config config;
         config.pooling_allocation_strategy(PoolAllocationConfig());
         config.memory_init_cow(true);
+        /* Force bit-identical execution across build hosts. The Wasm spec
+           leaves NaN payload bits and relaxed-SIMD lowering
+           implementation-defined, so the same module may return different
+           bits on e.g. aarch64-darwin and x86_64-linux. builtins.wasm
+           results flow into eval caches and derivations shared fleet-wide,
+           where one divergent bit yields divergent store paths. Upstream
+           leaves NaN canonicalization off because it costs ~3.6x on a
+           float-heavy kernel (NixOS/nix#15380 discussion); evaluation-time
+           plugins are string/AST-shaped, so determinism wins here. */
+        config.cranelift_nan_canonicalization(true);
+        config.wasm_relaxed_simd_deterministic(true);
         return Engine(std::move(config));
     }();
     return engine;
